  "query_preview": "..\nSaturday through Sunday night: Weather trends cooler and drier for \nSaturday as the Inland Northwest lies in the wake of Friday\u2019s cold \nfront passage and exiting upper-level trough. 850mb temperatures on \nSaturday cool to 1-4C, translating to high temperatures in the upper \n50s to mid 60s. These temperatures are about 5-10 degrees below \naverage for this time of year.  A dry air mass combined with light \nwinds on Saturday brings moderate to high confidence for the \nnorthern valleys, the Palouse, and the central Idaho Panhandle \nvalleys to see frost Sunday morning, however increasing middle and \nhigh level clouds associated with an upper-level low digging into \nCalifornia may help moderate low temperatures a bit. The air mass \nslightly warms on Sunday with less cloud cover. Despite warmer high \ntemperatures, clearer skies and light winds brings another night of \nfrost/freeze conditions Monday morning. \n\nMonday through Friday: The flow aloft becomes more zonal across the \nPacific Northwest early next week, allowing temperatures to warm to \nnear seasonal averages (highs in the 60s to low 70s). An moisture \nplume associated with weather frontal systems moving into western \nCanada will bring cloud cover and a chance for precipitation to the \nmountains on Tuesday. This precipitation will be mostly \norographically enhanced under this zonal flow regime with limited \ndynamic forcing to support precipitation across the lowlands. The \nlatest National Blend of Models (NBM) guidance highlights a 70 to 80 \npercent chance for precipitation amounts greater than 0.50 inches \nand a 10 to 20 percent chance for north Idaho.  \n\nConsiderable model spread emerges in the ensemble guidance from \nWednesday through Friday, leading to lower confidence in the \nforecast for this period. Around 60% of ensembles favor the building \nof an upper-level ridge over the Pacific Northwest, which would \npromote dry conditions and temperatures near or slightly above \naverage. Conversely, the remaining 40 percent maintain a cooler, \nzonal pattern with the potential for a trough to traverse the \nregion. This alternate solution would keep temperatures cooler with \nlight precipitation, primarily focused over the mountains. /vmt",
